From 3c8da03bf9c5b9cb25c1d86e000cc34962428ccd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 18:28:05 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Orthotropic behaviours integrated by batches: rotations of
 all the points

The behaviours generated by the generic-parallel interface only provide
the rotations of batches: the orthotropic ones were refused by the
integration by batches. All the rotations of the batch path now process
all the points at once, with the rotation functions of MGIS taking views
(batch rotation function for a generic-parallel behaviour, point by point
for a generic one), which is also what the device will need.

- `MaterialArrays::getVersion`: number of the accesses allowing the
  modification of an array, so that values computed from it can be kept.
- `Material::computeRotationMatrices`: rotation matrices of the
  integration points in the form expected by MGIS (one matrix for an
  uniform rotation).
- `Material::getGradientsInGlobalFrame` and
  `getThermodynamicForcesInGlobalFrame`: values rotated at all the points,
  kept until the array or the rotation changes, in both layouts.
- `BehaviourIntegratorBase::integrateBatch` (replaces
  `integrateAllIntegrationPoints` and the overrides of the CRTP bases):
  rotation of the gradients before the integration, of the tangent
  operator blocks after it.
- Standard and FBar CRTP bases: `computeGradients` leaves the gradients
  in the global frame; the inner forces use the thermodynamic forces in
  the global frame of the material (both modes); no more refusal of the
  orthotropic generic-parallel behaviours.
- `computeMeanThermodynamicForcesValues` and the rotated evaluators use
  the values of the material (the evaluators now accept the component by
  component layout); exceptions are reported to the context.

Tests: new OrthotropicSaintVenantKirchhoffElasticity behaviour; the same
results to the last bit point by point and by batches for orthotropic
small and finite strain behaviours, with an uniform rotation and with
material axes varying from one point to another, generic and
generic-parallel (openmp, stlpar); values in the global frame compared to
the point by point rotations, recomputed after writes on the device, on
the host and a new rotation, in both layouts. 139/139.
